# Round prefillgroup (G), the landing A/B after T: the layer-first scheduler with the cross-layer wrap
# over pairs of batches against today's batch-first order, one binary, BLOOMERY_PREFILL_GROUP=2 against =1.
# Flow model (tools/flow, round flowb1, the wrap order of cardnext-design-report.md 1.1): lcg without the
# hot list, P 4096, B1+T 208.2 -> B1+T+G 256.8 [derived], +23.3 % [+22.3..+24.1] over the model's
# constants; the host union becomes ~90 % of the wall (63 of 70 ms a layer-batch). The B1 lease read the
# same union 1.3 % slower when its share of the wall rose 8 points (the named term union-duty, 68.43 /
# 67.55 ms): carried to a 17-point rise that is ~1.9 ms a layer-batch, -2.7 % on the effect [derived],
# so the band's low edge is +19.0. P 512 is one batch: G predicts 0 there and is not the term.
kind: ab
question: does the wrap scheduler hide the route under the union at P = 4096 on the lcg prompt, as
  the flow model's max(host, card) wall says?
term: pp tok/s @ P = 4096, lcg prompt, no hot list, A6000, plan (a), CED on - (G 2 - G 1) / G 1,
  paired by round
unit: %
predict: +19.0..+24.1
rounds: 2
sd: 1.1 % rig-log 09-25#v41-prefill-s14, two binaries' per-round pp ratios, one run's SD about 0.55
  and 1.1 %; the larger
condition: the prefillgroup binary on top of cardtile with the cross-layer wrap of cardnext 1.1 (the
  stock layer-first order without it reads +11.3 % [derived]; a per-layer wait_lb near the route in
  the G 2 arm means the wrap is missing), both arms in one lease through its lever,
  BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=2 just depth-gpu-ds41 4096 with the two lever arms, the order rotated each
  round, BLOOMERY_STEP_STATS=1 on both so wait_lb, enqueue_lb and union_lb print per arm; no row
  carries [cpu-busy] or [other-busy]. Command: BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=2
  BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='BLOOMERY_STEP_STATS=1 BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/prefillgroup-pp.card' just
  depth-gpu-ds41 4096 4096@BLOOMERY_PREFILL_GROUP=1. The prose prompt is not run: after T its card and
  host meet at P = 4096 (09-26#cardtile-ab), and its G term is derived from the corrected card_in
  (round cardinread) rather than measured
decide-in: keep G 2 as the default; lcg P = 4096 is host-bound (the union 63 of a 70 ms
  layer-batch): h3tile-b or host streaming is the next lever there
decide-below: read wait_lb and enqueue_lb: wait near the route (~20 ms) is a shared exchange event
  (F4), enqueue above ~8 ms is the queue; otherwise read union_lb against the G 1 arm - a slower
  union under the overlap is union-duty, a host clock term the model does not carry
decide-above: the union ran faster overlapped than alone: re-derive k(T) and the union's causes
  before sizing host streaming
